Khaata — Personal Financial Command Center
Day 42/60 — #60DaysOfClaudeAI
A single-file financial dashboard built for a student living on a family allowance — designed like a traditional Pakistani khaata (ledger book), not a generic expense tracker.
Features
Financial Health Score — a stamped, ledger-style score (0–100) blending savings rate, budget discipline, and needs-vs-wants balance
Money In / Money Out — log allowance and expenses by category
Budget — set per-category monthly limits with live progress bars
Savings Pot — a no-deadline rainy-day fund tracker
Cash Flow — 6-month income vs. expense trend (hand-built SVG bar chart)
Insights — auto-generated, rule-based observations on spending patterns
What-If Lab — sliders to simulate cutting a category and see semester-long savings
Calculators — 50/30/20 allowance planner + cash runway calculator
Reports — printable monthly summary
Learn — tips, a monthly checklist, resources, and ready-to-use Claude prompts
Tech
Pure HTML, CSS, and vanilla JavaScript — zero external libraries or CDNs
Fully offline-capable, works by just opening the file
Data persists in localStorage — nothing leaves the browser
Dark/light mode toggle, responsive down to mobile, printable report view
Design
Ledger/notebook aesthetic: ruled-paper background, navy-ink base, brass-gold accents, and a rotated "ink stamp" health score as the signature visual element — built around the idea of a student's hisaab-kitaab, not a SaaS dashboard template.
Run it
Just open Khaata-Financial-Command-Center.html in any browser. No install, no server, no dependencies.
Built as part of #60DaysOfClaudeAI — one polished single-file app a day.
